{"type":"event","event":{"type":"thinking_end","text":"The current implementation:\n- Iterates in dict order, picking the first ready node — not lexicographically smallest.\n- Infinite loop on cycles (no ValueError).\n- KeyError not raised for missing deps (actually `all(d in done for d in deps)` — missing deps would just never be in done, causing infinite loop instead of KeyError).\n- Doesn't mutate input currently? It reads only, so it doesn't mutate. But the new implementation should also not mutate.\n\nI need to implement:\n1. Kahn's algorithm with a heap for lexicographically smallest ready node.\n2. Validate deps: any dep not in graph → KeyError.\n3. Cycle detection: if processed count < len(graph), raise ValueError.\n4. Don't mutate input: use copies of adjacency/deps lists.\n\nImplementation:\n\n```python\nimport heapq\n\ndef schedule(graph):\n    remaining = {node: set(deps) for node, deps in graph.items()}\n    for node, deps in remaining.items():\n        for d in deps:\n            if d not in remaining:\n                raise KeyError(d)\n    ready = [node for node, deps in remaining.items() if not deps]\n    heapq.heapify(ready)\n    out = []\n    while ready:\n        node = heapq.heappop(ready)\n        out.append(node)\n        for other, deps in remaining.items():\n            if node in deps:\n                deps.discard(node)\n                if not deps:\n                    heapq.heappush(ready, other)\n    if len(out) < len(graph):\n        raise ValueError(\"cycle detected\")\n    return out\n```\n\nWait — the \"discard node from all deps\" loop is O(V) per pop, making it O(V^2 + E). That's fine for typical use, but Kahn's with reverse adjacency is O(V + E log V)... Actually with a heap it's O((V+E) log V). The reverse adjacency approach:\n\n```python\ndef schedule(graph):\n    indeg = {}\n    dependents = {node: [] for node in graph}\n    for node, deps in graph.items():\n        for d in deps:\n            if d not in graph:\n                raise KeyError(d)\n        remaining = set(deps)\n        indeg[node] = len(remaining)\n        for d in remaining:\n            dependents[d].append(node)\n    ...\n```\n\nHmm, careful with duplicate deps — using set dedupes. But remaining must be a new set so we don't mutate input lists (deps could be a list; iterating is fine, we never mutate). We only read deps. We don't mutate graph. Good.\n\nThe O(V^2) \"scan all deps each step\" is simpler; but the reverse-adjacency version is O(V + E + k log V), simplest asymptotically efficient. Let me do the heap + reverse adjacency.\n\nEdge cases:\n- Empty graph → return [].\n- Self-loop: dep == node → cycle → ValueError. With dedupe: remaining = {node}, indeg 1, never ready → ValueError. Good.\n- Duplicate deps: set() dedupes so indeg counts unique deps. When d processed, dependents[d] contains node once (since set iteration). Good.\n\nKeyError message: should raise KeyError for absent dependency. `raise KeyError(d)` — fine.\n\nLet me write the code and test."}}
